WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.560
Music

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:33.560
I was very anxious to get into the tropics as a naturalist. I yearned to see it.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.560
And I had already run programmes with people from the London Zoo.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.560
And there was a lovely man there called Jack Lester who was in charge of the reptiles,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.560
who had spent years as a banker, I think, in Sierra Leone.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.560
But he was a passionate naturalist, a great expert on the snakes of West Africa.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:58.560
And he and I cooked up the idea that the London Zoo might send a collecting expedition

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.560
to go and collect more from reptiles, particularly snakes and birds.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.560
I said that if the Zoo would do that, I would like to come with a camera

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.560
until I get permission from the BBC to film how it was done.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.560
And in those days, film cameras, 16mm cameras, were relatively primitive.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.560
And the film was very insensitive.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.560
So we wouldn't be able to film much in the West African forest.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.560
That's quite different from the openness of annas where you see giraffes and things.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.560
In the forest it was very dark. So you couldn't take long-focus lenses.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.560
You couldn't get close-ups of things. But that wouldn't matter.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.560
We'd film Jack who would find something, collect it, and then you would dissolve it in the studio.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.560
And there it would be live. And then Jack would explain all the details.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.560
And I said to Jack, this is something that no one's ever seen before.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.560
And there was this particular bird, scientifically called Picathartes gymnosepheles.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.560
That is the picture of a very rare bird, the white-necked Picathartes.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.560
This is the story of another expedition which went to West Africa

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:09.560
to look for the bird, to film it on its nest, and to try and bring it back alive.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.560
We landed at Freetown on the coast of Sierra Leone.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.560
Jack had caught his fleeting glimpse of the bird near a village 100 miles away in the protectorate.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.560
So as soon as we could, we set off in our lorry along the dusty red earth roads

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.560
which cut through the thick tropical bush on our way to the interior.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.560
Landing in Africa, or landing anywhere in the tropics, particularly the wet tropics,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.560
for the very first time is an unforgettable experience.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.560
I mean, the air, which is heavy with moisture, is also heavy with perfumes of various sorts.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.560
The sheer smell of the tropics is something you feel, which is extraordinarily evocative.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.560
And we landed on a small little grass strip in Freetown, which didn't have a metal strip,

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.560
it was only a small little toccata on a grass strip.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.560
And I remember walking on the hedgerow and seeing hibiscus, and then in the hibiscus

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.560
there was a chameleon and praying mantis, and you suddenly was aware of the great fecundity,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.560
the vast richness of topical nature.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.560
We took our places behind the hide, and from it we had a clear view of the boulder

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.560
and the two nests stuck on it.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:30.560
And then suddenly we saw one, a few yards away in the twilight of the bush, preening itself.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.560
This was enormous excitement.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.560
And then, deathlessly, we waited to see if it would actually fly to its nest and resume incubating.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.560
Then up it fluttered onto the nest, and as it did so, the other parent flew across

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.560
and drove the first one away.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.560
This was a great thrill for us, for as this happened, we became the first Europeans

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.560
ever to see the white-necked picothartes on its nest.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.560
It is an interesting bird, and nobody had seen it before, but it really wasn't the point of the thing.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.560
The point of the thing was to show how a zoo went about collecting animals.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.560
I should add, perhaps, that zoos wouldn't do that now.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.560
This is, we're talking about 1954, so it's over 50 years ago.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.560
And then we thought that the natural world was healthy, full of animals.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.560
If an animal died in a zoo, well, you simply went out and got another.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.560
You don't do that anymore.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.560
This is a West African in Sierra Leone.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.560
Fascinating music.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.560
For a while the dance had been going on, I had been recording the music on my tape recorder.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.560
This, of course, was the object of a great deal of curiosity,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.560
and I always played the recording back and let the singers listen to themselves on a little earphone.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.560
Blank astonishment was always followed by huge grins of delight.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.560
I couldn't, of course, remember the dance.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:24.560
I couldn't, of course, record what she said, because I only had the one recording machine.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.560
Back in 1954, that was the first portable tape recorder in existence.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.560
It was a huge thing about that size, which carried, I think, either 10 or 12 U2 torch batteries, big torch batteries.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.560
There wasn't any other recording machine that was portable for tape.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.560
So it was wherever we went we were recording new things.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:54.560
And none of the people we met had ever seen or heard a tape recorder before.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.560
And they didn't really believe that what was in the box was able to record this.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:07.560
I played one piece, a very nice, this Belangio, it's a West African xylophone, and a very good player.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.560
He was the virtuoso, and he listened to it very critically, and he said,

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:16.560
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, it's just long, but that was an easy piece.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.560
That would be easy for it to learn.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.560
I'll now play something really difficult and see what it does then.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.560
So he then played a real virtuoso piece, very, very fast and so on.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.560
And he was astounded when he discovered that my little green box could even reproduce that.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.560
If Africa has a heart, this must be very close to it.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.560
For within a few miles of one another, there rise here two of its main arteries.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:50.560
A few miles north, it lies the source of the Congo River, which flows west down to the Atlantic Ocean.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.560
And this tiny brook at my feet is the infant Zambezi River.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.560
Soon the waters are lapping around Leolui, the capital of Boratsiland.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:11.560
And this man is a member of the entourage of the Litunga, the paramount chief.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:15.560
The people must move out to the hills on either side of the plain.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:22.560
And the departure of the Litunga for his wet season capital is the occasion for the greatest festival in the whole of Boratsiland.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:30.560
The annual migration of the Litunga and its court in Boratsiland is very well known, custom, because the Zambezi floods.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:38.560
And during flood time, the Litunga, the paramount chief, the king, lives on the top of a hill, a little village.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:48.560
And then the floods recede, and in due course the court, headed by the Litunga, returns back to the lowlands where their fields are and so on.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.560
So it's an annual migration, and it's an annual festivity.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.560
The Litunga, I mean, you know, his costumes he was, what he wore when he went to Ascot.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.560
So, I mean, you know, top hat and tails and the whole thing.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.560
The difficulty in filming it was that nobody was prepared to tell us when it was going to happen.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:13.560
And the Litunga, we had to sit outside his court, his big palace hut, day after day, asking, you know, will it be now?

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.560
And the Litunga said, well, that may not be mine.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.560
And I don't know how long we were there for, I expect my memory exaggerates, but we were certainly there for many days,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.560
waiting for this moment when the Litunga said, yes, tomorrow we'll go.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:34.560
And when you do go tomorrow, I mean, it was a big deal because all the palace stuff had to be shifted on these barges.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.560
The Litunga will travel in the royal barge, the Nalekwanda.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:48.560
On board it goes the palace safe.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:58.560
The nobleman, the Ndunas, who will paddle the barge, dress themselves with kilts of skins.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:05.560
On their heads, they must wear pieces of a lion's mane.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:12.560
Preparing it was difficult because they had a very big barge, the royal barge,

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.560
and it would only go down one particular tributary, which was big enough.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.560
And even that wasn't really big enough. So what they did was build a series of dams.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:25.560
So the dam would hold the water high enough to carry the barge.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.560
And then when the barge got there, they broke down the dam and with the swirch of the water, they moved on to the next dam.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:39.560
So it was quite a big proceeding. It took all day.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:47.560
My cameraman colleague, Geoffrey Mulligan and I, were fortunate enough to be invited by the Adamsons out to Kenya to see Elsa and her cubs.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:56.560
In the shade of the trees, Joy Adamson had her studio, for she herself is an artist whose beautiful drawings of African plants

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.560
and of costumes of some of the Kenya tribes are displayed in the museum in Nairobi.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:10.560
And as she sat in her studio, she could watch the procession of visitors who unknowingly posed for her.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:18.560
Elsa herself often came to this river to drink. And as we watched the baboons, we were hoping that she might arrive on this evening.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:25.560
When evening came, she still had not appeared. But that night, as we lay in our tents, we heard her.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:32.560
But the roars were not her normal call. She was fighting with a strange lioness whose tracks George had seen not far from camp.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:38.560
And the battle sounded a desperate one.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.560
Joy was distraught because she thought Elsa had been wounded in the fight and might not come back.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.560
And we were exhausted because we had been travelling overnight.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:55.560
And I went to sleep on the camp bed down by the river. And I was woken actually by a huge weight on my chest.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:11:05.560
And it was actually Elsa. And I looked up and I saw Elsa's jaws just above my face, snaggled with saliva, terrible herniatosis too, I don't mind saying.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:14.560
And I had no idea what to do. And before I could do anything at all, Joy, I heard Joy calling,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:26.560
Elsa, my leprin! And Elsa heaved herself up and moved across to Joy, who then fondled her as though she was a pussycat.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.560
Elsa really wasn't the trouble. She had by this stage three cups in fact.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.560
One of them, the young male, was very frisky and liked to play games.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:46.560
And Joy herself wore canvas guards on her feet because Jesper, the cupboard, would lie in ambush,

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:50.560
then come out and sweep these claws out and really savage you.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:59.560
And so you had to be very careful of Jesper. And you could see that in a few months' time,

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:05.560
he was going to be a real handful because he had no fear of human beings at all.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.560
And he thought it was a great game, you know, to lie around, bush and pounce on you.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:14.560
The extraordinary thing about Elsa was not that she was a table ioness,

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.560
but that she had gone out into the wild and found a mate and produced cubs.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:27.560
And then, and this is the remarkable thing about the Elsa story, that she brought the cubs back to show Joy.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:32.560
At least I say that, I don't know that she brought them back to show Joy, but she certainly brought them back.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:36.560
And Joy certainly saw them. And that was really very extraordinary.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:43.560
In Fairness and the Adamansons, they did not personify the lioness and treat her as a human being,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.560
as many people treat their pets.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:54.560
Their concern was precisely the opposite, that they would try to return Elsa to being a wild animal.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.560
I think that's actually impossible.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:05.560
But I suppose it's truer to say that she was a lioness in two worlds, which I think is the title Joy used, one time or another.

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:18.560
That cross on the top of a volcano wasn't a Christian cross.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:25.560
It was a symbol of a strange, pagan, rather frightening religion called a cargo cult.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:29.560
And in the Pacific, it happened in many different places.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:36.560
And in the island of Tana, nearly all the natives believe in this strange man who will come and will bring all the cargo.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.560
And they call him John Froman.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:45.560
We passed many crosses on the way, both large and small.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:51.560
But as we approached Sulphur Bay, we found the most extraordinary monument which we had seen so far,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.560
standing by itself under its own thatched shelter.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:02.560
On the right-hand side, there was a scarlet aeroplane, perhaps that which will bring the cargo.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:07.560
And on the other side, a cage containing an extraordinary winged rat.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:15.560
And in the center, John Froman himself, with a scarlet coat and a white European face.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:20.560
Cargo cults occurred a number of times over the past several hundred years or so,

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:24.560
ever since Europeans have been in the Pacific.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:29.560
And we now recognize what they took place in the 19th century, which we didn't realize what they were before.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:37.560
And they are a consequence of people who have not been seen western industrialized objects

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:41.560
and who pick up, let us say, a teaspoon.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:47.560
And who say, what is this? How could you chip this? How could you weave it?

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.560
How could you get it out of stone? They've never seen metal before.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:54.560
Europeans tell them that they come from across the seas.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:15:00.560
So the people think, well, if they've come, why don't they come to us?

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:05.560
And they think the Europeans have gotten away from the gods sending these things.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:11.560
And the excuses that the Europeans have, they say, if you want them, you'll have to work for us,

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.560
or you'll have to believe in our god.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:20.560
And so cults arose in which they said, Europeans are stopping us getting this cargo,

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.560
which is what they call it.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:26.560
And therefore we will get in touch with the gods.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.560
How will we do that?

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:32.560
And they look at the Europeans and they see that the Europeans do very strange things.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:38.560
They sometimes sit in front of a box, which makes funny noises, and talk into it.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:44.560
They sometimes sit at table and eat in a very curious way.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:46.560
Maybe they should have to do those things.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.560
So that's what the cargo cult leaders did.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:56.560
Numbers, men tell me that you have a radio.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.560
This is the leader of the cult.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.560
John Crumb has a radio, is that right?

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:08.560
Yes.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.560
You have a special radio, John Crumb.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.560
Yes.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:16.560
And what does he say on this radio?

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.560
He speaks English.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:22.560
John Crumb tells you on the radio that we will come?

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:24.560
Yes, he speaks English.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:26.560
Too many come.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.560
The first plane comes, too many come.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.560
Me and Master Jelani?

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.560
You speak me first time.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:39.560
They had a woman who would go into a trance and go into a special hut,

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:45.560
which had a huge aerial, as it were, a great tall mast, imitating the aerial that people used.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:49.560
And she would wind the flecks around her body and then fall on the ground in a trance.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:55.560
And as people do in trances, they spoke gibberish, really.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:17:01.560
And Numbers, the leader, would maintain that he could understand what she was saying.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:05.560
And he said that's what she said, predictions or whatever they were.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:12.560
And it's very difficult to talk sensibly to those people without being insulting.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:13.560
I mean, that's what they believed.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:21.560
And so you must be gentle about it and don't go in and say you're talking rubbish.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:24.560
Those men were dancing in an island in the South Seas.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:28.560
And we were watching them on our way to another island called Pentecost.

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:34.560
And we heard that on that island an even more spectacular ceremony was to take place,

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:38.560
one of the most spectacular of all ceremonies of the South Seas.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.560
We had no difficulty in finding out where the ceremony was to take place

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:48.560
because people who were preparing for it passed us all day long carrying bundles of vines.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:53.560
All we had to do was to follow them.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:18:00.560
Soon after dawn the following day, the festival began.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:06.560
Many of these people had come from the interior of the island many hours much distant.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.560
At least 20 of them will jump before the end of the day.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.560
Almost immediately, a young boy together with his two assistants

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:26.560
climbed into the tower to take up his position for the first dive.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.560
He was going to jump from one of the lowest platforms,

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:40.560
yet even so he would be over 40 feet from the ground.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:19:04.560
Without a pause, a second diver left the ranks of the dancers.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:09.560
The land diving ceremony in Pentecost had been photographed before by the National Geographic,

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:11.560
but had not been filmed before.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:16.560
So we were the first people to do that and we heard when it was going to happen

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:21.560
and we pitched up by canoe actually on the southern island on Ambrym

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:25.560
and finally made our way to the village and they were very happy that we should film it

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.560
and we gave them rewards and so on.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.560
The ceremony itself is rather mysterious.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:36.560
It's easy to, and perhaps not inaccurate to say it's an initiation ceremony

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:41.560
in which the boy shows that he is brave and an adult.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:46.560
What happens of course is that the lianas which are cut in the forest are actually rather curly,

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.560
so they've got a lot of give in them.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:56.560
The night before the ceremony, each of the platforms which stick out from the front of the tower

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:01.560
have the lianas going over the front of them and then dangling down the front

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:04.560
so that they could measure that they weren't too long,

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.560
because if they were too long of course he'd hit the ground full pelt.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:13.560
What in fact happens is that not only do the corkscrew lianas lengthen,

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:17.560
but the struts which hold out the platform, they collapse,

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:22.560
so the platform moves down like that, which is an added reduction of speed.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:28.560
So the jumper jumps, the lianas stretch, the platforms collapse,

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:31.560
and then he reaches the extent of his fall outwards,

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:34.560
and the lianas tighten and then pull him back,

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:38.560
so he lands on his back on the ground, which is carefully softened.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:40.560
So it's not as dangerous as it might seem.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.560
I didn't try it myself, I should hasten to add, and it's quite dangerous enough,

00:20:45.560 --> 00:21:01.560
but that's why nobody killed themselves on the occasion we showed it anyway.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:05.560
Madagascar, for the naturalists, is above all the land of the Lemur,

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:10.560
those strange ancestors and relations of the monkeys.

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:15.560
So we went down to the thick tropical rainforests on the eastern coast of Madagascar

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:17.560
to look for the imps.

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:22.560
Hardly anything had been filmed in Madagascar from a natural history point of view.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:27.560
Indeed, many of the animals had not even been photographed alive.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.560
I remember doing a research and finding out what lived there,

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.560
and the last colonial power controlling Madagascar was France,

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:40.560
so much of the literature, the scientific literature, is in French,

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:45.560
and it was illustrated by photographs of stuffed animals.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:49.560
And I remember the indria itself was a stuffed animal,

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:52.560
a very mangy sort of flea-ridden thing,

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:56.560
it looked horrible, bearing in mind how beautiful the actual animal is.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:01.560
So we were able to go there, and no matter what, if it was alive and moving,

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:06.560
nobody had filmed it before.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:11.560
We searched day after day, but still found no sign of them.

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:15.560
However, the forest was full of a variety of birds,

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:19.560
many of them especially interesting because they're found only in Madagascar.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:23.560
A recording of some of their songs would be worthwhile having,

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.560
and I could at least do that.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:30.560
So one morning I set out with a tape recorder.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:42.560
But this noise was no bird call.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:45.560
I had never heard anything like it before.

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:48.560
It must be the voice of the indus.

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:52.560
The song was so loud that it seemed impossible that the animals

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:55.560
could be more than 20 or 30 yards away.

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.560
But where were they?

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:00.560
The animals were there, but we couldn't see them.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:04.560
Then I had an idea. We had got its recorded voice.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:08.560
Perhaps if we played the recording in various parts of the forest,

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:11.560
we might persuade the indus, sitting concealed in the trees,

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:17.560
to sing in answer to the recording, and so reveal its presence.

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:31.560
This was different, yet it must be the voice of the indus.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.560
It could be nothing else.

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.560
They were replying to their recorded song with alarm calls.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.560
But where were they?

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.560
There, 40 yards away, farther than I'd guessed,

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:51.560
judging from the loudness of their calls, and 30 feet up in the tree.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:54.560
They were big creatures, even larger than I'd imagined,

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.560
at least three feet tall.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:05.560
And the tinny voice of the recorder seemed to hold them fascinated.

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:09.560
The technique is really a very well-known one to ornithologists.

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:12.560
Of course, they started by actually many birdwatchers,

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:14.560
of course, very skilled imitators of bird calls.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:16.560
They can whistle bird calls.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.560
And even hunters, of course, call up with special calls

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:22.560
when they call up their prey.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:25.560
So the technique of calling animals while making the noise

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:27.560
is a fairly antique one.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:30.560
I didn't know whether it would work for the indu or not,

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:33.560
but as I said on the commentary, it was the last ditch.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:37.560
Might as well try it, and it worked very well.

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:47.560
We went to Madagascar primarily to look for animals,

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.560
but we hadn't been there long before we became fascinated

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:52.560
and indeed enchanted by the people.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:56.560
This season, when the land is about to spring into life again,

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:58.560
is a time of deep significance,

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:00.560
the moment when with great festivities

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.560
the graves of the dead must be opened.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:18.560
The people of Madagascar worship their ancestors.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:21.560
It is the ancestors who prevent sickness or poverty

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:23.560
from overwhelming a family,

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:25.560
the ancestors who grant that a woman

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.560
shall be able to bear children.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:30.560
But the dead lead a miserable existence

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.560
in the silence and solitude of the tomb,

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:35.560
so they must therefore be brought out regularly

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:40.560
and entertained with music and with dancing.

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:42.560
There's a ceremony called the Retournement,

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:46.560
and it's perhaps the most famous ceremony in Madagascar.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.560
Anybody who reads anything about travels in Madagascar

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.560
will come across references to the Retournement.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:56.560
It in fact only happens in the Meriner country.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.560
The Meriner, one of the dominant people in Madagascar,

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:05.560
whose ancestors came not from Africa but from Southeast Asia.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.560
And it is the Meriner people,

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:10.560
who included the royal family,

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:13.560
who practiced Retournement.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:17.560
And it's a way of celebrating the ancestors,

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.560
remembering the ancestors,

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:23.560
and caring for the ancestors, and very touching.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:28.560
And after all, we, in our prayers,

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:31.560
think of our ancestors too.

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:33.560
The ceremony is an expensive one.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.560
The musicians must be paid,

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:39.560
fresh silk wrappings provided for the corpses,

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.560
everyone must wear new clothes,

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.560
and a great deal of food must be prepared

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.560
to feed the assembled company

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:48.560
and to be offered to the ancestors.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:52.560
The whole of a family's income may be devoted to it.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:55.560
But it is a feast in which everyone is happy to take part.

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:59.560
It is a matter of pride and honour to hold such a ceremony.

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:03.560
A man who failed to do so would be despised

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:06.560
and be called unfit to bear his father's name.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:11.560
For it is taboo to let people lie in a tomb uncared for.

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:15.560
Only the ancestors of dogs, they say, are left like that.

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:17.560
The program's got a big audience,

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:19.560
and people said they liked it.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:21.560
And I think it's a common characteristic,

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:23.560
not unexpected,

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:25.560
that you should be interested in what happens

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.560
beyond your own community.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:30.560
People always have, people who always enjoyed travellers' tales,

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:33.560
and that's what we were doing, quite simply,

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:37.560
showing how other people lived and what other people did.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:41.560
And the more different it is from what you did in Western Europe,

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:57.560
the more interested people are.

00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.560
All the programs that you've just seen,

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:02.560
from Africa to the Far East, to Australia,

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:21.560
you can see again on bbc.co.uk slash BBC4.
